\section{Units are converted, and that is the point}{

A covariate is only interchangeable across sources if it arrives in the same
units, so four of these are converted on the way out rather than passed
through:

\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{CHL}} — COBALT writes \code{chlos} in kg m-3. Multiplied by 1e6 to
give mg/m3, which is what Copernicus and the satellite products use.
\item \strong{\code{NO3}, \code{PO4}, \code{O2}} — written in mol m-3, multiplied by 1000 to
give mmol/m3.
\item \strong{\code{BOTO2}} — \code{btm_o2} is mol kg-1, multiplied by 1e6 to give
umol/kg, which is what oxygen per unit mass is conventionally reported
in and puts it on the same scale as \code{O2}'s mmol/m3. Note the basis:
this is \strong{per kilogram of seawater}, where \code{O2} is per cubic metre.
Seawater is about 1025 kg/m3, so the two are close but not equal, and
converting between them needs a density. That is why they are
deliberately not given the same name.
}

The conversion factor is in each entry's \code{scale}, so what was done to a
value is readable rather than buried in the reader.
}

\section{What is monthly and what is also daily}{

The NWA12 hindcast saves everything monthly. Its \strong{daily} output is
biogeochemistry only — \code{CHL}, \code{NO3}, \code{PH}, \code{PCO2}, \code{PHYC}, \code{MESOZOO} and
\code{BOTO2} — and carries no temperature, salinity, sea surface height, mixed
layer depth, ice or velocity at all. \code{daily} in each entry records that, and
\code{\link[=accessCEFI]{accessCEFI()}} refuses a daily request for a monthly-only variable rather
than returning an empty join.
}

\section{Primary production is not here}{

COBALT writes \code{intpp}, which is production integrated over the water column
in mol m-2 s-1. The package's \code{PP} is the satellite product's volumetric
rate in mg/m2/day. They are different quantities measured on different
bases, and giving them one name would make a nonsense of any comparison, so
CEFI simply has no \code{PP}. \code{MESOZOO} has the same shape of caveat — it is a
200 m integral, in mol m-2 — and is given its own name for that reason.
}

